            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="border border-[var(--color-line)] px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">Units</td>
              <td class="border border-[var(--color-line)] px-4 py-2.5">Units purchased</td>
              <td class="border border-[var(--color-line)] px-4 py-2.5">Units sold</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Investors may use SIPs to save up their money and then consider withdrawing. Depending on your goals, risk tolerance and other income sources, will determine which is the right option for you.</p>

      <h2>How much can I Withdraw?</h2>
      <p>There is no one answer to this as it will depend on your investment, costs, risk, how long you are withdrawing and actual rate of return. Think about your essential costs, other income, emergency funds and how long you need the money for. Try a few different amounts.</p>
      <p>Don't just go for a high amount as it may look like you'll have some money left. This can cause the balance to reduce quicker depending on the rate of return especially in the beginning.</p>

      <h2>Considerations Before Starting an SWP</h2>
      <p>Consider the following before starting regular withdrawals:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Market Volatility – The value of mutual funds can vary. During a period of negativity the balance may reduce.</li>
        <li>Amount Withdrawn – The higher the amount you withdraw the quicker your investment will decrease.</li>
        <li>Investment Horizon – Consider how long you may need the withdrawals for.</li>
        <li>Fund – Understand what the fund is investing in, what assets it has, risk and if it suits your needs.</li>
        <li>Liquidity – Have appropriate funds for emergencies.</li>
        <li>Tax – Tax will vary depending on the type of fund you are investing in, how long you have held it and what regulations apply. Please check what the current regulations are or speak to a qualified tax professional.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>An SWP will not remove the risk of investing and doesn't guarantee a fixed income. You should regularly review your withdrawals.</p>

      <h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
      <p>Avoid the following common mistakes:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Assuming guaranteed returns. The rate of return is just an assumption.</li>
        <li>Withdrawing large amounts. This can cause the balance to reduce quicker.</li>
        <li>Not considering inflation. The value may not be as much.</li>
        <li>Using high rates of return. This can make your plan look more sustainable.</li>
        <li>Ignoring costs. There will be costs of taxes, exit loads and expenses.</li>
        <li>Not reviewing. Consider your withdrawals if your costs or market changes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>FAQs</h2>
      <h3>What is an SWP Calculator?</h3>
      <p>An SWP Calculator is a tool that will give you an idea of how much you will have left in your mutual fund after regular withdrawals. You can enter how much you initially invested, how much you want to withdraw, what return you expect and how long you will be withdrawing.</p>

      <h3>Are returns guaranteed?</h3>
      <p>No. The returns from mutual funds are linked to the market. The rate of return is an assumption and can vary.</p>

      <h3>Can I withdraw a fixed amount each month?</h3>
      <p>You can set up a Systematic Withdrawal Plan on a mutual fund. This will allow you to withdraw a set amount at set intervals. However, this will depend on how much you have invested and what the fund allows. If there isn't enough in your corpus you may not be able to withdraw the full amount.</p>

      <h3>Will my units reduce?</h3>
      <p>Yes. Every time you make a withdrawal you will be selling units from your mutual fund. The amount of units sold will depend on how much you are withdrawing and the applicable NAV.</p>

      <h3>Are there any taxes?</h3>
      <p>As you are withdrawing you will be selling units from your mutual fund. There may be tax on the capital gains part. This will depend on the type of fund, how long you have held and applicable regulations. Tax can change so please check what the current regulations are or speak to a tax professional.</p>

      <h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
      <p>An SWP can be a great way to access your existing mutual fund investment. Using an SWP Calculator can help you understand how different amounts, rates of return and periods will affect your balance.</p>
      <p>Use realistic assumptions, play around with different scenarios and regularly review your plan. Consider your costs, other income, risk and tax.</p>

      <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
      <p>This calculator is for educational purposes and will only give you an estimate. It is not a guarantee of returns and is not financial, tax or investment advice. Actual results may vary due to market fluctuations, tax, exit loads, expense ratios and other factors. You may want to speak to a qualified financial adviser before making any investment decisions.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
